>Or using a game mechanism for a purpose other than what is likely
>intended in an attempt to circumvent the DevTeam's intentions and to
>simplify the game.  

Okay, first of all, where is it written that anything a player may do
must be a result of the conscious intentions of the devteam? The
devteam creates a construct--the game--in which certain things are
allowed and certain things are disallowed. Within this construct, the
player is free to roam around and do anything he wishes, try any
strategy, work out any solution to the problems the game presents to
him. Some unforseen consequences benefit the player (e.g., the shop
bug) or work against the player (e.g., summoning explosions). If
serious enough, the devteam labels it as a bug and fixes it. Some
unforseen consequences are considered negligable enough to leave in
the game, with the understanding that players will use them.

>To me, the latter is where #naming of gems to catch curses fits in.  I
>doubt the ability to #name gems is a bug in itself.  It serves a valid 
>purpose.  However, it's also clear that the intent of this feature is
>not to allow the player to artificially inflate the number of items in
>his or her inventory.  

Why not? It's probably not intended specifically by the #naming
feature, but what's wrong with concept of adding items to your
inventory in order to reduce the possibility that random curses will
strike something important? This seems to me to be a particularly
normal and valid solution to a problem that the game throws at you.
Random curses? Okay, add to the number of items that can be randomly
cursed.

>There's really no solution that's not punitive or inconsistent.  Still,
>it's obviously a case of twisting the game mechanics in a direction they
>weren't designed to go for the benefit of the player.  It's abusive.  

Again, why do you come to this absurd conclusion? Some people in this
forum argue that the result actually works *against* the player
(because he's carrying extra weight in order to get a *slightly*
smaller chance that his BoH or unihorn will be unblessed). Why is such
a big deal being made about something that, to my knowledge, has never
been controversial before?

Perhaps you'd like to tell us what items *should* be allowed in our
inventory in the Endgame. Perhaps only armor and weapons? Should we be
allowed wands--or maybe just attack wands? Is there a certain point
after which we must put our mirrors and tin openers away? Is carrying
the Candelabrum abusive once you don't need it any more because it may
absorb a curse?
>
>But then again, do abuses that are this minor really matter?  Doubtful.

You don't say.

